winter sports fans have been hitting the  slopes at the Garmisch classic ski area  for almost 90 years now every year close  to a million visitors enjoy the 40  kilometres of ski runs down these three  mountains most stick to the snow on  store-bought skis and boards but a  little workshop in a  garmisch-partenkirchen suburb offers a  more personal alternative you don't have  to buy your skis in a shop here you can  custom build your own bending the edges  for their own skis this weekend our nine  men and one woman doctors teachers  students and an engineer move of  ahankhah it bends everywhere except  where I wanted to anyone can buy skis in  a shop but custom building your own is  great what you get depends on how much  effort you put into it if you do a bad  job you get a bad ski and if you put  lots of work into it you can get a  really good ski Florian Baumgartner is  one of the workshops co-founders the  idea came by chance and demand is 5 even  if nothing comes easy for the  do-it-yourselfers once everything's been  sanded down and the surfaces are  completely finished they begin to  realize what they've just spent two days  doing those are all the individual parts  that have to be prepared and then  tomorrow they'll be holding the finished  product then you'll see their eyes open  wide for now though their eyes are  focused on the design  the graphic aspect is an important one  but a question of individual taste  I think it'll end up too busy if you  start laying purple peacock feathers in  and my experience that never looks good  but this way it looks great let's look  at version number one cambering only and  no active rocker now it's a matter of  tuning the skis performance the  cambering or arching has to be carefully  adjusted then layer four layer of  fiberglass mats are glued to the core  with the top sheet coming last the  layers are not pressed together in  pre-manufactured molds like they are for  factory made skis here a foil is drawn  out in a vacuum and the ski is laminated  and baked in a special oven  the odds of hidden since we set the  shape length and camber in each pair for  each customer individually we'd have to  make new moulds for every one that would  drive the prices sky-high so we work  with a vacuum because the vacuum sac  stretches out evenly over the whole ski  the skis have been cured in the oven now  there's one last step left for the next  morning  they cut out the profiles sand them down  and then spit them up  once a person has made a pair of skis  with their own hands they tend to hold  out of them  at last the time has come to try them  out the skis are presented inside the  shop but the skis have to dry thoroughly  before their owner can strap them on and  take off now comes a long tedious wait  before I can finally try them out next  weekend but I'm glad I made them this  exceeded all my expectations
 

